# Lock the robot camera's focus whenever it is plugged in or the board boots.
#
# Install:
#   scp scripts/99-farmcam-focus.rules unoq:/tmp/
#   ssh unoq 'echo <sudo-pw> | sudo -S install -m 644 /tmp/99-farmcam-focus.rules \
#       /etc/udev/rules.d/ && echo <sudo-pw> | sudo -S udevadm control --reload-rules'
# Test without unplugging anything:
#   ssh unoq 'echo <sudo-pw> | sudo -S udevadm trigger --action=add --subsystem-match=video4linux'
#
# WHY. The QHM-999RL powers up with continuous autofocus ENABLED, and focus/AF are driver-side
# controls with no persistence — they reset on every replug and every boot. Measured on this
# camera at the robot's real mounting height, sharpness (Laplacian variance) across
# focus_absolute 0..1023 ran 28 to 324: an 11.6x spread with a clean unimodal peak at 272. A
# hunting lens therefore intermittently destroys exactly what the tube detector gates on
# (_TUBE_MIN_FWHM_PX, _TUBE_MIN_SIGMA), for no reason visible in the frame.
#
# A udev rule is enough here, which had to be checked rather than assumed: the usual warning is
# that webcam controls reset when an application reopens the device, and this app opens the
# camera at startup. Verified on the board — AF off + focus 272 survived a full
# `docker restart motor-control-main-1`, so the rule firing at plug time is not undone later.
#
# ATTR{index}=="0" IS LOAD-BEARING. A UVC camera exposes two video4linux nodes with the same
# name: the capture node (index 0) and a metadata node (index 1). Only the capture node carries
# these controls, so without this the rule also fires on the metadata node and v4l2-ctl fails
# there with "unknown control". Matching by vendor:product rather than by video number is the
# other half — /dev/video indices are NOT stable on this board (the camera was video2 one day
# and video0 the next, with the Qualcomm Venus codec taking the other pair).
#
# 272 IS SPECIFIC TO THE CURRENT CAMERA HEIGHT. Re-measure with scripts/lock_focus.sh if the
# mount moves, and update this file — a stale value here is worse than autofocus, because it is
# silent.

# TWO SEPARATE RUN LINES, NOT ONE COMMAND WITH TWO -c FLAGS. This is the whole reason the first
# version of this rule silently did nothing.
#
# v4l2-ctl bundles multiple -c flags into a single VIDIOC_S_EXT_CTRLS transaction, and
# focus_absolute is flagged INACTIVE while autofocus is on. So the combined write fails
# atomically and applies NEITHER control:
#
#     $ v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 -c focus_automatic_continuous=0 -c focus_absolute=272
#     focus_absolute: Input/output error
#     VIDIOC_S_EXT_CTRLS: failed: Input/output error
#     $ v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 -C focus_automatic_continuous
#     focus_automatic_continuous: 1        <- autofocus still ON
#
# udev reported this only as "failed with exit code 255" in the journal, and the camera simply
# kept hunting. Split into two calls it works every time: turn AF off first, which makes
# focus_absolute active, then set it.
#
# READBACK TRAP, same family as the gain/--get-ctrl one in fix_camera.sh: while AF is ON,
# focus_absolute still reports the last value written to it. It is a stale echo, not the lens
# position. Always confirm focus_automatic_continuous is 0 before believing focus_absolute.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0806", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0806", ATTR{index}=="0", RUN+="/usr/bin/v4l2-ctl -d /dev/%k -c focus_automatic_continuous=0"
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0806", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0806", ATTR{index}=="0", RUN+="/usr/bin/v4l2-ctl -d /dev/%k -c focus_absolute=272"
